// Headline: which states count fewer road deaths as speeding-related, and how much of that is coding?
//
// Finding (2022-2024): Florida counts 11.1% of its road deaths as speeding-related (1,116 of 10,061), the lowest of
// any state; the rest of the US counts 30.3%. Of the vehicles its crash reports recorded at 10+ mph over the posted
// limit, Florida coded 36.5% as speeding (750 of 2,054). The 11 other states that record speed and limit for half or
// more of vehicles coded 56.6% (California, 1,023 of 1,806) to 78.6% (Virginia, 679 of 864). Florida also records
// fewer fast vehicles (15.6% of vehicles with speed and limit recorded, Virginia 25.6%), so the coding is part of the
// gap, not all of it.
// If I knew that, I would compare states on deaths in crashes with a recorded fast vehicle, only among states that
// record speed and limit for half or more of vehicles (Texas: 7.0%), and ask the state's FARS analyst how the flag is
// coded before ranking it. Who acts: state highway safety
// offices, NHTSA's FARS staff, anyone ranking states on speeding deaths.
// Hypotheses written before the queries: units/ltools/outputs/ltp-rubric/HYPOTHESES.md (work repo, commit 63719d76).
//
// This file stands alone: it reads the two FARS tables it needs (fars.vehicles, fars.crashes in MotherDuck, loaded by
// load_fars.sql from NHTSA's 2019-2024 FARS files) and defines only what the headline uses. fars.malloy holds the full
// model for deeper questions; the tests in the work repo (outputs/ltp-part2/headline-standalone) check that every
// figure here equals the same figure computed from fars.malloy.

// One row per state and year from the vehicle file. Speed is a vehicle field, so the coding question is asked of
// vehicles; the state comes from the crash the vehicle was in.
source: fast_vehicles is motherduck.table('fars.vehicles') extend {
  join_one: crash is motherduck.table('fars.crashes') extend { primary_key: crash_key } on crash_key = crash.crash_key
  dimension:
    // "Fast" = recorded travel speed 10+ mph over the posted limit. Null when either is blank, so a blank speed is
    // never counted as fast or as not fast. 10 mph keeps clear of rounding in officers' speed estimates.
    over_limit_by_10 is travel_speed_mph >= posted_speed_limit_mph + 10
} -> {
  group_by:
    `year`
    state_name is crash.state_name
  aggregate:
    vehicles_n is count()
    // Flaw: travel speed is blank for about 60% of vehicles nationally (Texas records speed and limit for 7.0%, Florida
    // 88.5%), so every share below is of the vehicles whose speed was written down, not of all vehicles.
    speed_known_n is count() { where: travel_speed_mph is not null and posted_speed_limit_mph is not null }
    fast_n is count() { where: over_limit_by_10 }
    // The vehicle's own FARS speeding flag (speeding-related as coded on the crash report). Flaw: an unknown flag
    // counts as not speeding (Nebraska 2021-2024 30.8-38.0% unknown, Ohio 2022-2023 17.5-18.8%).
    flagged_n is count() { where: over_limit_by_10 and speeding_related }
    // Rest of the US in the same year, carried on each row so a state filter cannot break the comparison.
    rest_fast_n is all(count() { where: over_limit_by_10 }, `year`) - count() { where: over_limit_by_10 }
    rest_flagged_n is all(count() { where: over_limit_by_10 and speeding_related }, `year`) - count() { where: over_limit_by_10 and speeding_related }
}

// One row per state and year from the crash file: deaths, and deaths in crashes the report flags speeding-related.
source: deaths_by_state_year is motherduck.table('fars.crashes') -> {
  group_by: `year`, state_name
  aggregate:
    // Deaths = sum of fatalities per crash (people killed within 30 days), never a count of crashes.
    deaths_n is fatalities.sum()
    speeding_n is fatalities.sum() { where: speeding_related }
    // Deaths in crashes with any vehicle recorded 10+ mph over the limit: the count that does not depend on the flag.
    fast_deaths_n is fatalities.sum() { where: vehicle_10_over_limit }
}

#" Speeding coding by state and year: of vehicles recorded 10+ mph over the limit, the share coded speeding, beside the speeding share of deaths.
#(agent) Grain: state x year. Pool years with a where on `year`; every measure sums over the rows kept. Compare states only with 100+ fast vehicles in the years kept and speed and limit recorded for half or more of vehicles.
source: speeding_coding is fast_vehicles extend {
  // Join guard: both sides are one row per state and year, so join_one on both keys cannot fan out.
  join_one: deaths_side is deaths_by_state_year on state_name = deaths_side.state_name and `year` = deaths_side.`year`

  measure:
    #" Vehicles recorded 10+ mph over the limit.
    vehicles_10_over is fast_n.sum()
    #" Of those, vehicles FARS flags as speeding-related.
    vehicles_10_over_flagged is flagged_n.sum()
    // The coding rate: how often a vehicle the report itself shows as fast is coded speeding. Differences between
    // states here are differences in coding, since every vehicle in the denominator was recorded fast.
    #" Share of 10+-over vehicles flagged speeding-related.
    # percent
    flagged_share_of_10_over is vehicles_10_over_flagged / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0)
    // Wilson 95% interval: stays inside 0-100% and holds for small counts, unlike the plain normal interval.
    #" Lower end of the Wilson 95% interval on the flagged share.
    # percent
    flagged_share_low_95 is (flagged_share_of_10_over + 1.9208 / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0) - 1.96 * sqrt(flagged_share_of_10_over * (1 - flagged_share_of_10_over) / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0) + 0.9604 / nullif(vehicles_10_over * vehicles_10_over, 0))) / (1 + 3.8416 / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0))
    #" Upper end of the Wilson 95% interval on the flagged share.
    # percent
    flagged_share_high_95 is (flagged_share_of_10_over + 1.9208 / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0) + 1.96 * sqrt(flagged_share_of_10_over * (1 - flagged_share_of_10_over) / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0) + 0.9604 / nullif(vehicles_10_over * vehicles_10_over, 0))) / (1 + 3.8416 / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0))
    #" The flagged share in the rest of the US, like for like.
    # percent
    rest_of_us_flagged_share is rest_flagged_n.sum() / nullif(rest_fast_n.sum(), 0)
    // Two-proportion z test against the rest of the US (vehicles treated as independent; several fast vehicles in one
    // crash share a report, so the test is a little generous).
    z_flagged is (flagged_share_of_10_over - rest_of_us_flagged_share) / sqrt(nullif(((vehicles_10_over_flagged + rest_flagged_n.sum()) / nullif(vehicles_10_over + rest_fast_n.sum(), 0)) * (1 - ((vehicles_10_over_flagged + rest_flagged_n.sum()) / nullif(vehicles_10_over + rest_fast_n.sum(), 0))) * (1 / nullif(vehicles_10_over, 0) + 1 / nullif(rest_fast_n.sum(), 0)), 0))
    #" Flagged share against the rest of the US: beyond chance or not.
    flagged_share_vs_rest_of_us is pick 'Too few to test' when vehicles_10_over < 10 pick 'Not tested' when z_flagged is null pick 'Higher, beyond chance' when z_flagged > 1.96 pick 'Lower, beyond chance' when z_flagged < - 1.96 else 'Within chance'
    // Coverage: the share of vehicles with both speed and limit recorded. Low coverage means the fast vehicles are a
    // small sample whose selection depends on when officers write speed down.
    #" Share of vehicles with both travel speed and posted limit recorded.
    # percent
    speed_and_limit_known_share is speed_known_n.sum() / nullif(vehicles_n.sum(), 0)
    // Denominator: vehicles with BOTH a recorded travel speed and a posted limit. Many states leave speed blank
    // (Texas records it for 7.0% of vehicles, Florida 88.5%), so a state's fast vehicles are a sample of its crashes
    // whose size and selection depend on how often officers record speed.
    #" Of vehicles with a recorded speed and limit, the share recorded 10+ mph over.
    # percent
    fast_share_of_recorded is fast_n.sum() / nullif(speed_known_n.sum(), 0)
    // The official FARS measure: deaths in crashes the report flags speeding-related, over all deaths. It moves with
    // the flag rate (flagged_share_of_10_over) as well as with how fast people drive.
    #" Share of deaths in crashes FARS flags speeding-related.
    # percent
    speeding_share_of_deaths is deaths_side.speeding_n.sum() / nullif(deaths_side.deaths_n.sum(), 0)
    #" Deaths.
    deaths is deaths_side.deaths_n.sum()
    #" Deaths in crashes flagged speeding-related.
    speeding_deaths is deaths_side.speeding_n.sum()
    #" Deaths in crashes with a vehicle recorded 10+ mph over the limit (the count that does not depend on the flag).
    deaths_with_fast_vehicle is deaths_side.fast_deaths_n.sum()

  // Minimum count: views compare a state only with 100+ fast vehicles in the years kept. Under that, its flagged
  // share has a 95% interval wider than about +/- 10 points; it stays in the drill-down table.

  #" How often is a fast vehicle coded speeding? By state, 2022-2024 pooled, states with 100+ fast vehicles, highest share first.
  #(agent) Texas, Louisiana and New Jersey top this list but record speed for under 30% of vehicles; for a like-for-like comparison read `where_speed_is_recorded`.
  view: how_often_is_a_fast_vehicle_coded_speeding is {
    where: `year` >= 2022
    group_by: state_name
    aggregate:
      vehicles_10_over, vehicles_10_over_flagged, flagged_share_of_10_over, flagged_share_low_95, flagged_share_high_95
      flagged_share_vs_rest_of_us, speed_and_limit_known_share
    having: vehicles_10_over >= 100
    order_by: flagged_share_of_10_over desc
  }

  // Coverage guard: the like-for-like comparison keeps states recording speed and limit for half or more of their
  // vehicles, so the fast vehicles are most of the state's crashes rather than a small, selected subset.
  #" Same, only states that record speed and limit for half or more of vehicles (2022-2024): the like-for-like comparison.
  view: where_speed_is_recorded is {
    where: `year` >= 2022
    group_by: state_name
    aggregate:
      vehicles_10_over, vehicles_10_over_flagged, flagged_share_of_10_over, flagged_share_low_95, flagged_share_high_95
      speed_and_limit_known_share, fast_share_of_recorded, speeding_share_of_deaths
    having: vehicles_10_over >= 100 and speed_and_limit_known_share >= 0.5
    order_by: flagged_share_of_10_over desc
  }

  // Tested before the query (H-F3): across the 25 comparable states the speeding share of deaths correlates 0.514
  // with the flagged share and 0.426 with the fast share, but without Florida the order reverses (0.285 and 0.345).
  // So this view shows the pattern state by state; it does not support a general "the flag drives the count" claim.
  #" Does a state's speeding share of deaths follow its coding or its fast driving? By state, 2022-2024, comparable states.
  view: does_the_speeding_count_follow_the_coding is {
    where: `year` >= 2022
    group_by: state_name
    aggregate:
      speeding_share_of_deaths, flagged_share_of_10_over, fast_share_of_recorded, deaths, speeding_deaths
      deaths_with_fast_vehicle, vehicles_10_over
    having: vehicles_10_over >= 100
    order_by: speeding_share_of_deaths
  }

  // Flaw named here: 2024 is NHTSA's Annual Report File, not final (earlier years rose 0.5-0.7% when finalised).
  // North Carolina's rise from 63.9% (2022) to 88.3% (2024) is beyond chance while its deaths in crashes with a fast
  // vehicle did not change beyond chance (634 and 598): its speeding-related count rose with the coding.
  #" Did the coding change? Flagged share by year, 2019-2024, for Florida, Virginia and North Carolina (2024 not final).
  view: did_the_coding_change is {
    where: state_name = ('Florida' | 'Virginia' | 'North Carolina')
    // Year as text so tables and the chart axis show 2019, not 2,019.
    group_by: crash_year is `year`::string, state_name
    aggregate: vehicles_10_over, flagged_share_of_10_over, rest_of_us_flagged_share
    order_by: crash_year, state_name
  }

  #" Drill-down: every state, then each year: fast vehicles, flagged share with interval, speed recorded, and deaths.
  view: state_by_year is {
    group_by: state_name
    aggregate: vehicles_10_over, flagged_share_of_10_over, speed_and_limit_known_share, speeding_share_of_deaths
    nest: by_year is {
      group_by: crash_year is `year`::string
      aggregate:
        vehicles_10_over, vehicles_10_over_flagged, flagged_share_of_10_over, flagged_share_low_95
        flagged_share_high_95, speed_and_limit_known_share, deaths, speeding_deaths, deaths_with_fast_vehicle
      order_by: crash_year
    }
    order_by: vehicles_10_over desc
  }
}
